{"text":"Recent research supports combining sequential models with global item relations. [GaGNN (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2024.120623) combines individual and group transition graphs; [LS4SRec (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3723173) uses a global behavior graph to supply collaborative signals under sparsity. These support the motivation for a training-only transition branch, but do not establish that a sparse retrieval ensemble improves SASRec on this benchmark.

Text and behavioral relations require distinct treatment. [Sequence-level Semantic Representation Fusion (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.18166) reports limitations of directly combining text and ID embeddings. [PRISM (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.11846) identifies similarity bias in sequential attention: similar items can overshadow useful heterogeneous relations. Earlier [substitute/complement research](https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.08839) treats those relationships as separate supervised prediction targets; its review and relationship supervision exceeds the available task inputs. Lexical overlap alone therefore cannot establish accessory compatibility.

Practical inference from these findings: test separate SASRec, directed transition, and title/description TF-IDF scores. [TF-IDF](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/feature_extraction.html#text-feature-extraction) supplies sparse lexical features without pretrained weights. Aggregate transitions from recent history, normalize scores, shrink low-support edges, and cap lexical contributions when behavioral evidence is absent. Treat these fusion rules as hypotheses, not published guarantees. Compare transition-only, lexical-only, and combined additions using the pinned macro validation HR@10 across all four categories; inspect support and history-length slices before adopting adaptive weights."}
